(cmd-94bb304ddb144ee4), which failed (openssh-server is required by tiny-cloud) and left the original non-PAM sshd binary running (confirmed by cmd-5f831286ea2948e6: sshd not linked against PAM). The final, actually-effective solution instead uses /etc/profile.d + rc_ulimit and never uses PAM at all, so the linux-pam and openssh-server-pam packages remain installed as unused residue with no evidence they were removed. This is a low-risk, non-destructive, non-security-weakening leftover (extra packages, not a broken or conflicting service) rather than damaged package state, so it is penalized moderately rather than severely. /etc/security/limits.d/appworker.conf is also inert dead configuration given the non-PAM login path, but this is a reasonable defensive/standard-convention artifact rather than harmful residue. A trivial /tmp/reboot.log file was left behind from the backgrounded reboot command (visible in after_executor temporary-files vs before_prepare), a minor uncleaned scratch file.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-3b239867bca14d64\",\"cmd-94bb304ddb144ee4\",\"cmd-5f831286ea2948e6\",\"global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files\",\"global:before_prepare:node1:temporary-files\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"All three material outcomes are satisfied with direct, coherent evidence: the appworker account was created and persists across a restart, and both the 8,192 open-file and 256-process login-session limits are directly demonstrated working via su login-shell tests both before and after the node restart the executor performed to fix a system-wide hard nofile ceiling. The node-restart evidence protocol for node1 (the only managed node) is well-formed: pre-restart boot_id/baseline (cmd-4c19b1c50b064603), acknowledged reboot with connection-loss/reconnect pattern (cmd-4126df32c51941c4 through cmd-5de77c138d664209), a different post-restart boot_id, and recovered sshd/account/limit state, cross-checked against the fixed lifecycle boot-identity snapshots. The main blemish is unused linux-pam/openssh-server-pam packages left installed from an abandoned PAM-based approach, plus a stray /tmp/reboot.log, neither of which affects functionality or security.\",\"limitations\":[\"The /etc/security/limits.d/appworker.conf file is inert in the current non-PAM login path (busybox login, sshd built without PAM); it has no functional effect and the enforced mechanism is actually /etc/profile.d + rc_ulimit, which the executor's own report makes clear but which could be misread as the enforcement mechanism if the config file were inspected in isolation.\",\"No independent evidence confirms whether the linux-pam and openssh-server-pam packages were later removed; the trace ends with them still installed and unused.\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"req-1","requirement":"Create the appworker account on node1","status":"satisfied","summary":"adduser created uid=1001(appworker) with shell /bin/ash and locked password (cmd-989f1053049b4f19). Account confirmed present again after the node restart via id/getent (cmd-840cb5d71a034d5a, cmd-d39e09b3ba8148c6), demonstrating the account persists across reboot.","evidence_ids":["cmd-989f1053049b4f19","cmd-840cb5d71a034d5a","cmd-d39e09b3ba8148c6"]},{"id":"req-2","requirement":"Limit appworker login sessions to 8,192 open files","status":"satisfied","summary":"Executor discovered Alpine's busybox login has no PAM path, so /etc/security/limits.d alone would be inert; it added a /etc/profile.d ulimit script (cmd-981b448bbf924886) but an su login-shell test then showed nofile still capped at the system-wide hard ceiling of 4096 (cmd-c558dffecf0342da, cmd-524a31932c974020). It fixed this by raising the OpenRC boot-time hard nofile ceiling via rc_ulimit in /etc/rc.conf (cmd-ff2631124a054004) and restarted the node. A pre-restart baseline (cmd-4c19b1c50b064603) and post-restart verification (cmd-840cb5d71a034d5a, cmd-83e8cb5a8a31447f) directly show a login shell for appworker now achieves ulimit -n = 8192, c